      "created_at": "2018-10-29T22:55:50Z",
      "updated_at": "2018-10-29T22:55:50Z",
      "body": "I did some work drafting a privacy policy, but I realized that the SVN or Git credentials could be considered personal information.  I reviewed #307, and I think we can make the following assertions about how we use the credentials:\r\n- Moonshine itself does not save the credentials\r\n- The credentials will be passed to user-configured third-party applications.  These applications may save the credentials.\r\n- The user is not required to enter credentials - they can save the credentials with the third-party application directly, or they can use an external source control application.\r\n\r\n@rat-moonshine, please verify whether the code is consistent with these assertions.\r\n\r\nAnother case that I considered was the \"This is great!\" and \"I'm having some problems\" interfaces.   However, these interfaces do not request any personal information or account information from the user - instead the requests are forwarded to the Twitter, Facebook, or GitHub websites.  So, I don't think this needs any special attention in the privacy policy.\r\n\r\nIf anyone else thinks of other collected data that may need to be considered for the privacy policy,please let me know.  \r\n",
      "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
      "pin": null,
