      "created_at": "2018-10-30T07:40:46Z",
      "updated_at": "2018-10-30T07:40:46Z",
      "body": "I have checked through SVN and Git process on using the credentials, I confirm Moonshine do not stores any credential data to its local storage or neither to any server. While in case of Git, Moonshine provides option to re-use entered credential for the same application session - the entered credential stores to the memory as long Moonshine session is running, but I think this is irrelevant to an extent too. \r\n\r\nThe Git authentication window is suppose to come if user do not have any credential already stored to the system keychain. It seems like once any Git command _push_ command executes with a credential, it stores to the keychain, and next time you don't even need to provide any credential. I seen the behaviour in Windows, too.\r\n\r\nThe bottom-line is, by executing any SVN or Git command with given credential through Moonshine, the credential may store to the system keychain (by SVN/Git application) but Moonshine has no control over it.",
      "author_association": "COLLABORATOR",
      "pin": null,
